Fixes that NEST (pinned in scripts/build-nest.sh) needs to compile as static libraries with MinGW-w64 (MSYS2 UCRT64). Applied only by
scripts/build-windows.sh, with `git apply` onto a clean checkout of the pin; the Linux build never sees it. Each hunk is explained here.

nestkernel/mpi_manager.h (two hunks)
  gethostname() is a Winsock function on Windows (declared in <winsock2.h>, and it fails until WSAStartup ran), so the non-MPI
  MPIManager::get_processor_name() did not compile. It only fills the "processor name" string of the kernel status, so on Windows it asks
  for the computer name with GetComputerNameA (kernel32), declared by hand: <windows.h> would otherwise be pulled into every NEST file
  through this header and its macros (min, max, ERROR, ...) collide with NEST's names.

Not a patch, but part of the same port (scripts/build-windows.sh, engine/CMakeLists.txt): MinGW lays out bit-fields like MSVC by default, which breaks
NEST's compile-time size checks (SpikeData == 8 bytes, TargetData == 12); everything that includes NEST headers is built with -mno-ms-bitfields.

diff --git a/nestkernel/mpi_manager.h b/nestkernel/mpi_manager.h
index 56c752b..4b0faf7 100644
--- a/nestkernel/mpi_manager.h
+++ b/nestkernel/mpi_manager.h
@@ -28,6 +28,10 @@
 
 // C includes:
 #include <unistd.h>
+#ifdef _WIN32
+// gethostname() lives in Winsock (needs WSAStartup); the computer name is all we want. Declared by hand so that <windows.h> does not enter every NEST file.
+extern "C" __declspec( dllimport ) int __stdcall GetComputerNameA( char*, unsigned long* );
+#endif
 #ifdef HAVE_MPI
 #include <mpi.h>
 #endif
@@ -612,7 +616,12 @@ MPIManager::get_processor_name()
 {
   char name[ 1024 ];
   name[ 1023 ] = '\0';
+#ifdef _WIN32
+  unsigned long len = 1023;
+  GetComputerNameA( name, &len );
+#else
   gethostname( name, 1023 );
+#endif
   return name;
 }
 
